WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.264
A California city says average emergency response time fell by fifteen percent after a dispatch change.

2
00:00:06.264 --> 00:00:15.872
Is that evidence that the change worked, or could the result reflect which calls were counted, an unusually quiet month, or a new definition of response time?

3
00:00:15.872 --> 00:00:19.946
Statistics helps separate the measured claim from the causal story.

4
00:00:19.946 --> 00:00:21.845
This is study preparation only.

5
00:00:21.845 --> 00:00:25.153
It awards no score, course credit, or transfer credit.

6
00:00:25.153 --> 00:00:29.013
The city and numbers in this episode are invented for practice.

7
00:00:29.013 --> 00:00:30.666
Start by defining the data.

8
00:00:30.666 --> 00:00:33.912
A population is the full group we want to understand.

9
00:00:33.912 --> 00:00:36.301
A sample is the part actually observed.

10
00:00:36.301 --> 00:00:45.365
If the question concerns all emergency calls in a year but the report analyzes two hundred calls from April, those two hundred calls are the sample.

11
00:00:45.365 --> 00:00:47.508
A parameter describes a population.

12
00:00:47.508 --> 00:00:49.407
A statistic describes a sample.

13
00:00:49.407 --> 00:00:53.510
The true annual mean response time is a parameter, usually unknown.

14
00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:57.001
The April sample mean is a statistic used to estimate it.

15
00:00:57.001 --> 00:01:00.492
Similar words do not make the quantities interchangeable.

16
00:01:00.492 --> 00:01:04.411
A variable records a characteristic for each observational unit.

17
00:01:04.411 --> 00:01:06.922
Response time in minutes is quantitative.

18
00:01:06.922 --> 00:01:10.046
Call type, such as medical or fire, is categorical.

19
00:01:10.046 --> 00:01:16.170
A quantitative variable can be discrete, like the number of calls, or continuous, like elapsed time.

20
00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:19.416
Clear units and definitions are part of the variable.

21
00:01:19.416 --> 00:01:24.071
"Response time" is incomplete until we know when the clock starts and stops.

22
00:01:24.071 --> 00:01:27.255
Sampling method affects what a sample can represent.

23
00:01:27.255 --> 00:01:33.319
In a simple random sample, every possible sample of a stated size has an equal chance of selection.

24
00:01:33.319 --> 00:01:40.484
A stratified sample divides the population into meaningful groups, such as call type, then samples within each group.

25
00:01:40.484 --> 00:01:47.282
A cluster sample randomly selects whole groups, perhaps dispatch zones, then studies calls in the chosen zones.

26
00:01:47.282 --> 00:01:51.875
A systematic sample might select every twentieth call after a random start.

27
00:01:51.875 --> 00:01:55.121
Convenience samples use cases that are easy to reach.

28
00:01:55.121 --> 00:01:59.286
Voluntary response samples attract people who choose to participate.

29
00:01:59.286 --> 00:02:00.450
Both can be biased.

30
00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:10.984
If a city surveys only residents who completed an online complaint form, it may miss residents without reliable internet access and residents who gave up before completion.

31
00:02:10.984 --> 00:02:15.516
An observational study records what happened without assigning treatments.

32
00:02:15.516 --> 00:02:18.333
An experiment deliberately assigns conditions.

33
00:02:18.333 --> 00:02:27.703
A city that compares response times before and after a software launch has an observational before-and-after comparison unless assignment was controlled.

34
00:02:27.703 --> 00:02:32.603
Confounding occurs when another factor changes alongside the factor of interest.

35
00:02:32.603 --> 00:02:38.421
Staffing, weather, call severity, and reporting rules can all confound the software comparison.

36
00:02:38.421 --> 00:02:41.486
Descriptive statistics organize what was observed.

37
00:02:41.486 --> 00:02:46.329
Consider five response times: eight, nine, ten, eleven, and twenty-two minutes.

38
00:02:46.329 --> 00:02:47.984
To find the mean, add them.

39
00:02:47.984 --> 00:02:49.149
The total is sixty.

40
00:02:49.149 --> 00:02:50.068
Divide by five.

41
00:02:50.068 --> 00:02:51.723
The mean is twelve minutes.

42
00:02:51.723 --> 00:02:55.463
The median is the middle ordered value, which is ten minutes.

43
00:02:55.463 --> 00:02:59.999
The unusually long twenty-two-minute call pulls the mean above the median.

44
00:02:59.999 --> 00:03:02.819
Quartiles divide ordered data into four parts.

45
00:03:02.819 --> 00:03:09.746
The interquartile range describes the spread of the middle half and resists extreme values better than the range.

46
00:03:09.746 --> 00:03:13.792
A box plot displays the median, quartiles, and potential outliers.

47
00:03:13.792 --> 00:03:17.102
Variance and standard deviation use every observation.

48
00:03:17.102 --> 00:03:22.926
Standard deviation describes a typical distance from the mean in the variable's original units.

49
00:03:22.926 --> 00:03:28.872
A standard deviation of three minutes says more directly than a variance of nine squared minutes.

50
00:03:28.872 --> 00:03:34.083
Two districts can have the same mean response time but different standard deviations.

51
00:03:34.083 --> 00:03:37.883
The less consistent district poses a different policy problem.

52
00:03:37.883 --> 00:03:40.213
A percentile states relative position.

53
00:03:40.213 --> 00:03:46.895
If a response time is at the ninetieth percentile, about ninety percent of observed times are at or below it.

54
00:03:46.895 --> 00:03:52.044
That does not mean the response was ninety percent correct or ninety percent faster.

55
00:03:52.044 --> 00:04:00.320
For public service, the median describes the middle case, while the ninetieth percentile draws attention to slower cases near the tail.

56
00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:03.078
Distributions have shape, center, and spread.

57
00:04:03.078 --> 00:04:06.266
A symmetric distribution balances around its center.

58
00:04:06.266 --> 00:04:14.726
A right-skewed distribution has a longer tail toward larger values, common when most calls are handled quickly but a few take much longer.

59
00:04:14.726 --> 00:04:18.772
In a right-skewed distribution, the mean often exceeds the median.

60
00:04:18.772 --> 00:04:21.959
Report both when one number could conceal the shape.

61
00:04:21.959 --> 00:04:24.044
Probability describes uncertainty.

62
00:04:24.044 --> 00:04:26.128
An outcome is one possible result.

63
00:04:26.128 --> 00:04:27.968
An event is a set of outcomes.

64
00:04:27.968 --> 00:04:30.175
Probability ranges from zero to one.

65
00:04:30.175 --> 00:04:33.547
A probability of zero means impossible under the model.

66
00:04:33.547 --> 00:04:35.631
One means certain under the model.

67
00:04:35.631 --> 00:04:39.064
Neither statement proves that the model matches reality.

68
00:04:39.064 --> 00:04:43.172
Suppose twenty of one hundred sampled calls exceed fifteen minutes.

69
00:04:43.172 --> 00:04:50.651
The empirical probability of a sampled call exceeding fifteen minutes is twenty divided by one hundred, or zero point two.

70
00:04:50.651 --> 00:04:52.062
That is twenty percent.

71
00:04:52.062 --> 00:04:57.395
It estimates a broader rate only if the sample and measurement process are appropriate.

72
00:04:57.395 --> 00:05:00.951
The complement of an event means the event does not occur.

73
00:05:00.951 --> 00:05:12.293
If the estimated probability of exceeding fifteen minutes is zero point two, the estimated probability of not exceeding fifteen minutes is one minus zero point two, or zero point eight.

74
00:05:12.293 --> 00:05:16.830
A probability distribution pairs possible values with their probabilities.

75
00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:21.367
For a discrete count, the probabilities must be nonnegative and total one.

76
00:05:21.367 --> 00:05:26.210
Expected value is a long-run weighted average, not a promise for the next case.

77
00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:29.148
Now examine two quantitative variables together.

78
00:05:29.148 --> 00:05:32.085
A scatter plot places one variable on each axis.

79
00:05:32.085 --> 00:05:35.451
Look for direction, form, strength, and unusual points.

80
00:05:35.451 --> 00:05:41.693
Positive association means larger values of one variable tend to accompany larger values of the other.

81
00:05:41.693 --> 00:05:46.221
Negative association means larger values tend to accompany smaller values.

82
00:05:46.221 --> 00:05:50.689
Correlation measures the strength and direction of a linear relationship.

83
00:05:50.689 --> 00:05:53.381
It ranges from negative one to positive one.

84
00:05:53.381 --> 00:05:57.604
Values near positive one indicate strong positive linear association.

85
00:05:57.604 --> 00:06:01.827
Values near negative one indicate strong negative linear association.

86
00:06:01.827 --> 00:06:08.069
Values near zero indicate little linear association, but a strong curved relationship can still exist.

87
00:06:08.069 --> 00:06:11.373
Correlation has no units and is sensitive to outliers.

88
00:06:11.373 --> 00:06:13.638
It also does not establish causation.

89
00:06:13.638 --> 00:06:19.329
Ice cream sales and heat-related calls may rise together because temperature influences both.

90
00:06:19.329 --> 00:06:26.795
Removing one unusual district can change the correlation substantially, so inspect the scatter plot before summarizing it.

91
00:06:26.795 --> 00:06:33.404
A least-squares regression line predicts an output from an input by minimizing the sum of squared residuals.

92
00:06:33.404 --> 00:06:41.115
Suppose a simple model predicts response time in minutes as eighteen minus one point five times the number of available units.

93
00:06:41.115 --> 00:06:44.787
With six units, multiply one point five by six, giving nine.

94
00:06:44.787 --> 00:06:46.500
Subtract nine from eighteen.

95
00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:49.193
The predicted response time is nine minutes.

96
00:06:49.193 --> 00:06:53.783
The slope is negative one point five minutes per additional available unit.

97
00:06:53.783 --> 00:06:59.474
The intercept, eighteen minutes at zero available units, may not be operationally meaningful.

98
00:06:59.474 --> 00:07:04.982
An intercept can be mathematically necessary without being a sensible real-world scenario.

99
00:07:04.982 --> 00:07:07.980
A residual is actual value minus predicted value.

100
00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:14.712
If the actual response took eleven minutes and the model predicted nine, the residual is positive two minutes.

101
00:07:14.712 --> 00:07:18.261
Positive means the actual value lies above the prediction.

102
00:07:18.261 --> 00:07:22.729
A curved residual pattern signals that the linear model misses structure.

103
00:07:22.729 --> 00:07:26.401
Prediction within the observed input range is interpolation.

104
00:07:26.401 --> 00:07:30.807
Prediction far beyond that range is extrapolation and carries more risk.

105
00:07:30.807 --> 00:07:39.558
If data cover two through eight available units, predicting twenty units assumes the same linear pattern continues where it was never observed.

106
00:07:39.558 --> 00:07:41.149
Return to the civic claim.

107
00:07:41.149 --> 00:07:47.085
Last quarter, two hundred calls had a mean response time of forty minutes and a median of thirty.

108
00:07:47.085 --> 00:07:52.654
This quarter, one hundred fifty calls had a mean of thirty-four and a median of thirty-two.

109
00:07:52.654 --> 00:07:57.672
The mean fell fifteen percent because six divided by forty is zero point one five.

110
00:07:57.672 --> 00:08:01.222
Yet the median rose by two minutes, and the caseload fell.

111
00:08:01.222 --> 00:08:07.953
The accurate conclusion is limited: the measured mean was lower in this quarter under the reported definition.

112
00:08:07.953 --> 00:08:19.336
To claim that dispatch software caused improvement, investigate sampling, call mix, missing data, clock definitions, staffing, weather, ordinary variation, and the distribution of times.

113
00:08:19.336 --> 00:08:22.824
One correct percentage cannot answer all those questions.

114
00:08:22.824 --> 00:08:23.804
Three questions.

115
00:08:23.804 --> 00:08:28.951
First, what is the difference between a population parameter and a sample statistic?

116
00:08:28.951 --> 00:08:33.669
Second, why might a city report both the median and the ninetieth percentile?

117
00:08:33.669 --> 00:08:36.242
Third, what does a positive residual mean?

118
00:08:36.242 --> 00:08:44.507
First answer: a parameter describes the full population, while a statistic describes the observed sample and may estimate the parameter.

119
00:08:44.507 --> 00:08:53.500
Second answer: the median shows the middle case, while the ninetieth percentile reveals performance among relatively slow cases near the upper tail.

120
00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:58.848
Third answer: the actual observed value was greater than the regression model predicted.

121
00:08:58.848 --> 00:09:05.549
Artifact: write a one-page audit of a real chart or numerical claim from a California local-government report.

122
00:09:05.549 --> 00:09:14.565
Identify the population, sample, variables, sampling risk, distribution shape if available, center, spread, and any correlation or regression claim.

123
00:09:14.565 --> 00:09:22.179
Recalculate one reported percentage, name one plausible confounder, and state the strongest conclusion the evidence supports.

124
00:09:22.179 --> 00:09:28.210
Source note: Original study audio based on public learning materials, without quoting source prose.

125
